
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether you own credentials or delegate them.

  - [1mEmail + password, server sessions[0m — you own the whole flow: password hashing (argon2), session cookies, password
    reset, email verification. No external dependency, works offline in dev. The cost is that you're now responsible for
    credential security forever, and reset/verification needs an email sender.
  - [1mOAuth / social login only[0m — delegate to Google or GitHub. No passwords to store, no reset flow, no verification
    email. But every dev and test environment needs a real provider or a stub, and you've tied signup to an external
    account people may not want to use.
  - [1mBoth[0m — most flexible, roughly the sum of both workloads. Classic YAGNI trap at this stage.

  For the API itself: I'd use session cookies rather than JWTs. Cookies are revocable server-side, which JWTs are not
  without rebuilding the stateful part you were trying to avoid — and for a browser app talking to your own API, they're
  strictly simpler.

  [1mMy recommendation: email + password with server sessions.[0m No external dependency for a project that's just starting,
  and it keeps dev and test environments hermetic. I'd stub the emailer in development rather than wire up a real
  provider on day one.
